WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.844 --> 00:00:03.011
(humming)

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.910
- [Narrator] In train and
subway stations all over Japan,

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.960
you're likely to hear a fully composed

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.860
seven-second jingle that is uniquely

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.900
written for that station.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.150
And if you're one of the millions

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.860
of Tokyo metro riders

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.850
You've probably heard one of these jingles

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.501
composed by Minoru Mukaiya.

00:00:32.501 --> 00:00:35.084
(lively music)

00:00:48.953 --> 00:00:51.370
(soft music)

00:00:52.428 --> 00:00:55.011
(upbeat music)

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.530
- [Narrator] Over time,
Mr. Mukaiya shifted

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.480
his focus to his other passion-trains-

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.173
and formed a company
that builds simulators.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.420
In this world of trains,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:32.500
he found a new outlet for his music

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.250
and got to work composing train melodies.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.485
Like this one

00:01:36.485 --> 00:01:39.818
(Shibuya Station music)

00:01:43.762 --> 00:01:46.429
(pensive music)

00:02:19.894 --> 00:02:22.477
(Ochiai music)

00:02:25.946 --> 00:02:29.029
(Takadanobaba music)

00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:36.267
(Monzen-Nakachō music)

00:02:42.542 --> 00:02:45.125
(Waseda music)

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.030
- [Narrator] Having found a
marriage of his two passions,

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.363
today, Mr. Mukaiya considers
himself a lucky man.

